-- Seed the free-resource catalog: 12 skills, one verified set each.
--
-- These fill the dead end in frontend/lib/plan.ts, whose per-archetype spine
-- ends in a bare skill string -- "statistics -- the hypothesis tests behind the
-- figure you reproduced" -- with no link, no course and no book behind it. A
-- student who already knew where to learn statistics did not need the
-- instruction; one who did not was told to learn statistics.
--
-- They also give the roadmap something a 9th grader can start this week. Of 61
-- live high-school opportunities, 4 have a usable future deadline and 31 are
-- locked behind a grade they have not reached, so the only "unlock" an
-- opportunity-only sequencer can offer a freshman is a birthday. Finishing a
-- free course is a real prerequisite instead.
--
-- HOW THESE WERE CHOSEN. Candidates were proposed per skill, then each was
-- checked by an independent verifier that fetched the URL and tested three
-- things: it loads and is the resource claimed, the learning is GENUINELY free
-- (not a trial, not free-to-audit-pay-for-the-certificate, no payment details),
-- and it teaches the assigned skill rather than something adjacent. Verifiers
-- were told to prefer rejecting, because approving a dead or paywalled link
-- puts it in front of a student with no money. Six candidates were rejected:
-- two as too advanced, two as teaching an adjacent skill, two on verification.
--
-- free_evidence quotes what each page actually says. That column exists because
-- an audit of this product's 35 hand-authored opportunity rows against each
-- program's own website found ONE accurate and retired nine. A catalog for
-- students with $0 cannot carry an unchecked "free".

insert into public.learning_resources
    (title, host, url, kind, skill_key, skill_label, opportunity_field,
     effort, prerequisite, why_this_one, is_free, free_evidence,
     verified_at, verified_note, added_by)
values
    ('Machine Learning Crash Course: Classification (accuracy, precision, recall, ROC/AUC)', 'Google for Developers', 'https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/classification', 'course', 'evaluation', 'evaluation — learning to measure whether an AI feature actually works, not just whether it runs', 'cs', 'One or two evenings — Google states "Estimated module length: 70 minutes"; plan 2-3 hours if you do every exercise', 'The module header lists "Introduction to Machine Learning," "Linear regression" and "Logistic regression" as prior modules, but the metrics pages are readable on their own — Google states "Each Machine Learning Crash Course module is self-contained." A student with no ML background should skim the…', 'This is the vocabulary rung. Before a student can argue that their AI feature works, they need to know why "it was right 95% of the time" is often a meaningless sentence, and this module is the clearest free explanation of that at a high-school reading level. The page on metrics literally works the example: "for heavily imbalanced datasets, where one class appears very rarely, say 1% of the time, a model that predicts negative 100% of the time would score 99% on accuracy, despite being useless." The math on the metrics pages is arithmetic only — fractions and division — and there are two embedded multiple-choice practice exercises that make you compute precision and recall by hand. It is…', true, 'Page loads in full with no login, no paywall and no signup prompt; footer reads "licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 License." Nothing on the page offers or requires a purchase.', now(), 'Two honest caveats, neither disqualifying, but the first should change what the catalog links to. 1. LINK THE METRICS PAGE, NOT THE COURSE HOMEPAGE. The course-wide prerequisites page (developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/prereqs-and-prework) is discouraging and, for this skill, misleading. It states "You should be a good programmer. Ideally, you should have some experience programming in Python because the programming exercises are in Python," and lists linear algebra (tensor…', 'seed'),
    ('Machine Learning Crash Course: Datasets, generalization, and overfitting — Dividing the original dataset', 'Google for Developers', 'https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/overfitting/dividing-datasets', 'course', 'evaluation', 'evaluation — learning to measure whether an AI feature actually works, not just whether it runs', 'cs', 'One sitting — the page itself is a 10-minute section inside a 105-minute module; do the whole module over a weekend', 'None beyond the idea that a model is trained on examples. Basic arithmetic. No coding required to read it.', 'This is the single idea that separates "it runs" from "it works," and it is a 10-minute read. It teaches training/validation/test splits and why a score measured on data the model already saw is not evidence of anything: "The only fair test of a model is against new examples, not duplicates," and it warns that "the more you use the same test set, the more likely the model closely fits the test set" — the trap a student falls into the first time they tune a prompt or a model against the same 20 examples over and over. It has "Check your intuition" and "Check your understanding" interactive questions, so the student gets feedback without a teacher. Do this immediately after the…', true, 'Loads fully to an anonymous fetch with the exercises and full text; same Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 licensing as the rest of the crash course; no account, no paywall, no trial.', now(), 'Approve, with three things a curator should know. 1. NARROW SCOPE — the real caveat. This page teaches the methodology half of evaluation (holdout splits, data leakage from duplicates, test-set wear-out). It does NOT teach metrics — no accuracy, precision, recall, ROC/AUC, and nothing LLM- or AI-feature-specific. On its own it does not cover the assigned skill; it covers the foundation the skill rests on. The proposing agent''s sequencing instruction ("do this immediately after the…', 'seed'),
    ('The LLM Evaluation Guidebook', 'Hugging Face', 'https://github.com/huggingface/evaluation-guidebook', 'textbook', 'evaluation', 'evaluation — learning to measure whether an AI feature actually works, not just whether it runs', 'cs', 'A weekend for the "Basics" section of each chapter; several weeks at a few hours a week if you work through the design…', 'Knowing what a language model is and having used one. No coding needed to read the Basics sections; the deeper chapters assume some Python if you want to run the tooling. Do the two Google modules above first so that "metric" and "test set" already mean something.', 'Once a student is building with an LLM instead of a classifier, precision and recall stop being enough — there is no single right answer to compare against. This guidebook is the best free writing on what you do instead, and it is organized exactly the way a learner needs: automatic benchmarks, human evaluation, and LLM-as-a-judge, each with its own Basics chapter, plus a chapter on "Evaluating the evaluator" — i.e. checking whether your judge is itself any good, which is the mistake almost every beginner makes. The prose is genuinely beginner-pitched: it defines terms as it introduces them (samples, "gold" references), uses spam classification as its running example, and compares…', true, 'Public GitHub repository, all content in plain Markdown files readable in the browser with no account. README: "If you don''t know anything about evaluation, you should start by the `Basics` sections in each chapter before diving deeper." Licensed CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. No paid tier, no gated chapters.', now(), 'One material thing the proposer omitted. Line 3 of the README — above everything else, in capitals — reads: "!! THIS GUIDEBOOK IS NO LONGER MAINTAINED. THE LATEST AND MOST UP TO DATE VERSION (as of Dec 2025) OF IT LIVES HERE: https://huggingface.co/spaces/OpenEvals/evaluation-guidebook" The proposer''s "free?" note quoted a line from further down that same README but said nothing about this banner. A curator should know the catalog would be pointing students at a version its own authors call…', 'seed'),
    ('Define success criteria and build evaluations (Claude developer docs)', 'Anthropic', 'https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/docs/test-and-evaluate/develop-tests', 'documentation', 'evaluation', 'evaluation — learning to measure whether an AI feature actually works, not just whether it runs', 'cs', 'An afternoon to read; a weekend to write your own first eval set of 20-50 test cases for something you built', 'Enough Python to read a short function (the examples are ~5-15 lines). Should come after the Hugging Face guidebook''s Basics sections, which supply the concepts this page assumes.', 'This is the how-to-actually-do-it page, and it is the closest thing on the free web to a checklist for "prove my AI feature works." It gives three design principles in plain language — "Design evals that mirror your real-world task distribution. Don''t forget to factor in edge cases!", "Structure questions to allow for automated grading (for example, multiple-choice, string match, code-graded, LLM-graded)", and "More questions with slightly lower signal automated grading is better than fewer questions with high-quality human hand-graded evals" — then shows four grading methods with complete code: exact match, cosine similarity, ROUGE-L, and LLM-as-judge on both a 1-5 Likert scale and a…', true, 'Anthropic''s public documentation site; the full page including all code examples returns to an anonymous fetch with no login wall, no paywall, and no trial signup. Verified freely readable without an account.', now(), '1) URL SHOULD BE CORRECTED BEFORE STORING. The proposed URL has a duplicated path segment — /docs/en/docs/test-and-evaluate/... — and does not serve directly; it 301-redirects (num_redirects=1) to the canonical https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/develop-tests. It resolves 200 today, but given this catalog''s stated history of dead links, store the canonical URL rather than relying on a redirect a future docs migration may drop. 2) Paid-API ceiling on part of the page. The…', 'seed'),
    ('Advanced High School Statistics (OpenIntro, 4th Edition)', 'OpenIntro (non', 'https://www.openintro.org/book/ahss/', 'textbook', 'statistics', 'statistics — the hypothesis tests behind reproducing a figure from a real paper', 'biology', '3-4 weeks at ~4h/week to work through the inference chapters (Ch. 5-8: confidence intervals, tests for proportions,…', 'None beyond Algebra 1. The book states it is ''designed to align with the AP Statistics curriculum and is widely used in high school and 2 year colleges.'' No calculus.', 'This is the only resource on the list written specifically for a high schooler with no college statistics behind them, and it is where the actual named tests live — one-sample and two-sample t-tests, chi-square for categorical data, and the p-value logic underneath them. Every other resource here assumes you already know what a null hypothesis is. Start here even if the biology sounds more exciting: you cannot judge whether a paper''s figure supports its claim until you can say what test produced the asterisk above the bar. It ships full worked solutions to the exercises, so a student with no teacher can still check their own work — which matters enormously for someone learning alone.', true, 'The book page offers three separate free downloads: ''FREE -- Advanced High School Statistics PDF (4th Edition)'' (the checkout lets you set the price to $0 to skip the optional contribution), a ''Direct download link for high school students'', and ''FREE -- 4th Edition PDF Best for Screen Readers''. Paid paperbacks ($25 b&w / $40 color) exist but are optional — the full text is in the free PDF.', now(), 'One correction the curator should make to the proposer''s blurb before publishing it. The proposer wrote: "It ships full worked solutions to the exercises." That overstates it. Appendix A contains solutions to the ODD-numbered exercises only (I counted exercise-number tokens across Appendix A: 209 odd vs 36 even, and the evens are sub-part/page artifacts). The complete solutions manual is gated — the book page says the "Adv. HS Statistics (4th Ed) exercise solutions" are "Available to Verified…', 'seed'),
    ('Statistical Thinking for the 21st Century + its R Companion', 'Russell A. Poldrack, Stanford University', 'https://statsthinking21.github.io/statsthinking21-core-site/', 'textbook', 'statistics', 'statistics — the hypothesis tests behind reproducing a figure from a real paper', 'biology', '4-6 weeks at ~3h/week reading the core book alongside the R companion, typing every code block yourself', 'No prior statistics and no prior programming required (the book ''assumes no prior statistics background''), though reading OpenIntro AHSS first will make it go much faster. You will need to install R and RStudio, both free.', 'This is the bridge between knowing a t-test and running one. The core book teaches hypothesis testing through simulation and resampling on real datasets rather than lookup tables, which is exactly the mental model you need to reproduce someone else''s figure — you shuffle the data, you see how often chance alone produces their effect. The free R Companion (https://statsthinking21.github.io/statsthinking21-R-site/) has a chapter on data visualization and a chapter on hypothesis testing in R, so you learn the plotting and the statistics as one skill instead of two. Crucially for this audience, the companion states most students in the author''s class ''have never programmed before'' and starts…', true, 'Both the core book and the R companion are published as open web books with source on GitHub under a Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial 4.0 license. There is no account, no paywall, and no trial — the full text renders in the browser.', now(), 'ONE PROPOSER CLAIM IS OVERSTATED — correct the blurb before publishing. The claim sells the R companion''s "chapter on hypothesis testing in R" as a co-equal half of the package. It is not. That chapter has only two sections (8.1 Simple example: Coin-flipping, 8.2 Simulating p-values), ~6,640 characters of body text and 14 code blocks — versus 68,158 B for the companion''s visualization chapter. It also renders a broken bookdown cross-reference as literal text on the page: "Section…', 'seed'),
    ('Shape Up: Stop Running in Circles and Ship Work that Matters (read Ch. 3 "Set Boundaries", Ch. 4 "Find the Elements", Ch. 14 "Decide When to Stop")', 'Basecamp', 'https://basecamp.com/shapeup', 'textbook', 'scoping', 'scoping — cutting an idea down to a one-week MVP without losing it', 'business', '2-3 hours for the three scoping chapters; a weekend for the whole book', 'None. No coding or business background needed — you only need an idea you''re tempted to over-build.', 'This is the only free resource that names the exact skill and gives it a method rather than a slogan. Chapter 3 teaches "appetite" — you decide the time budget FIRST and then design something that fits it: "Estimates start with a design and end with a number. Appetites start with a number and end with a design." That single inversion is what turns "my app idea" into "what can I actually build in one week." Chapter 14 gives the technique for the second half of the skill — not losing the idea — via "scope hammering": "Forcefully questioning a design, implementation, or use case to cut scope and finish inside the fixed time box," with the rule that you cut scope, not quality. It is written in…', true, 'Fetched basecamp.com/shapeup and two interior chapters (1.2-chapter-03, 3.4-chapter-13): the full chapter text renders with no paywall, no login, and no account prompt. The table of contents says "Start reading →" linking straight into Chapter 0.1; a print edition is sold separately at basecamp-goods.com, but the entire web book is readable free.', now(), 'Approved, but four things a curator should carry forward. 1. TIME-SCALE MISMATCH the student must translate. The book''s unit is a six-week cycle with a team of two or three; the assigned skill is a one-week solo MVP. The principle transfers cleanly, but nothing on the page does that division for the reader. If the catalog entry has a one-line "how to use this," it should say so explicitly: read appetite as "one week" and the cycle as "this week." 2. IT ASSUMES A TEAM in the parts outside the…', 'seed'),
    ('Practical Design: MVP Spec', 'Y Combinator', 'https://www.ycombinator.com/blog/practical-design-mvp', 'documentation', 'scoping', 'scoping — cutting an idea down to a one-week MVP without losing it', 'business', '45 minutes to read, plus about an hour to write your own one-page MVP spec — one evening total', 'None. Having one specific idea in mind makes it much more useful.', 'The shortest path from "I have an idea" to a written one-page scope you can hand to yourself on Monday. It forces the hardest question of this skill — what is the single function this product exists to perform — and then tells you to cut everything that is not that function: "A product is a manifestation of a function. A function is a purpose intended for a product. Without a function there would be no reason for a product to exist." It also draws the line beginners most often blur, between a prototype and a real first version: "A minimum viable product is a real product. Real users and real customers will use it and pay for it." Use it as the worksheet after Shape Up''s theory — it is by…', true, 'Fetched the article: it renders in full with no paywall, no signup wall, and no account required. It is a post on Y Combinator''s own public blog, not gated behind Startup School registration.', now(), 'Approving, but with three real caveats a curator should weigh — none of them is a hard-requirement failure, all of them are quality issues. 1. IT IS THIN. Roughly 800 words. It is a set of clean definitions (function vs. feature, product vs. prototype, minimum vs. maximum), not a method. It never actually walks a student through cutting a specific idea down. The proposer is honest about this — "by far the smallest time investment of the four" — and as the light worksheet after heavier theory…', 'seed'),
    ('User Interviews 101', 'Nielsen Norman Group', 'https://www.nngroup.com/articles/user-interviews/', 'documentation', 'user-research', 'user research — turning three real user conversations into one prioritized change', 'design', 'One evening to read and write your question list, then ~2 hours to run three 20-minute interviews', 'none', 'This is the single best starting point for the first half of the skill: actually getting three real conversations that produce usable data. It is short enough to read before dinner and concrete enough to act on the same week — it gives you the open-ended question forms a beginner needs (''Walk me through a typical day for you'', ''Tell me about the last time you [did something]'') and warns against the two mistakes every first-timer makes: treating the interview as a sales pitch and asking questions that assume an answer. NN/g has published free UX research articles since 1998 and is the most-cited practitioner source in the field, so a student who quotes it is standing on something a reader…', true, 'Fetched the URL directly: the complete article by Maria Rosala and Kara Pernice (published Sept 17, 2023, last reviewed July 15, 2026) returned in full with no paywall, no login, and no email gate. NN/g charges for its conferences, certification and research reports; the article library itself is open. The page states: ''Think of an interview as a research study, not a sales session or an informal conversation.''', now(), 'One real limitation a curator must not paper over: this resource covers only the first half of the assigned skill. It teaches how to conduct interviews. It does not teach how to analyze what you heard or how to prioritize a change — it mentions that interview data can feed UX artifacts like personas and journey maps, but gives no analysis or decision-making method. A student who reads only this ends up with three good conversations and no procedure for getting to "one prioritized change." The…', 'seed'),
    ('Affinity Diagramming: Collaboratively Sort UX Findings and Design Ideas', 'Nielsen Norman Group', 'https://www.nngroup.com/articles/affinity-diagram/', 'documentation', 'user-research', 'user research — turning three real user conversations into one prioritized change', 'design', '20 minutes to read, then a 2-hour sticky-note session on your own interview notes', 'none — but do it on notes from real conversations, not imagined ones, or the exercise is hollow', 'This teaches the exact second half of the assigned skill — the part almost no free resource actually covers. Most guides stop at ''go talk to users'' and leave the student holding three pages of messy notes. This article walks the full path in three steps: ''1. Generate ideas as sticky notes / 2. Organize notes into clusters or themes / 3. Prioritize clusters and next steps.'' Step 3 is literally ''turn the pile into one prioritized change.'' It needs nothing but sticky notes or a free Google Slides file, so a student with $0 and no software can do it tonight. Pair it directly with User Interviews 101: interview three people, then run this on the transcripts.', true, 'Fetched the URL directly: the full article by Rachel Krause and Kara Pernice (published April 26, 2024, last reviewed Aug 7, 2026) returned with no paywall or login. Opening definition as published: ''Affinity diagramming refers to organizing related observations, ideas, concepts, or findings into distinct clusters.''', now(), 'Three things a curator should carry forward. None of them sink the resource, but the first two should shape how it is presented to the student. 1. IT IS HALF THE SKILL, AND MUST BE SHIPPED AS A PAIR. The assigned skill is "turning three real user conversations into one prioritized change." This article teaches the conversations-to-change half only. It does not teach how to recruit anyone, what to ask, or how to avoid leading questions. On its own a student would have nothing to cluster. The…', 'seed'),
    ('Design Thinking Bootleg (the d.school method card deck)', 'Stanford University d.school', 'https://dschool.stanford.edu/resources/design-thinking-bootleg', 'pdf', 'user-research', 'user research — turning three real user conversations into one prioritized change', 'design', 'A weekend to read and try 4-5 cards; the deck is a reference you keep returning to for years', 'none', 'A ~90-page PDF of single-page method cards, each one a technique you can run in under an hour — which makes it the most usable format for a student who has 45 minutes after homework. For this specific skill it contains the whole pipeline end to end: interviewing for empathy, then ''Saturate and Group'' to get notes onto a wall, then the Point-of-View madlib that forces three conversations down to one statement (''1. We met: ___ 2. We were surprised to notice: ___ 3. We wonder if this means: ___''), then ''Why-How Laddering'' to pick the right altitude for the change you commit to — described in the deck as a way ''to flesh out varying user needs and find a middle ground that''s both meaningful and…', true, 'Fetched the page and then downloaded the PDF itself (4.2 MB, ~90 pages, from dschool.sfo3.digitaloceanspaces.com) with no account, no email, and no payment — the page offers only a ''Download Resource'' link with no price, signup, or trial mentioned. Verified the downloaded file''s contents, not just the landing page: it opens ''In your hands you hold a Design Thinking Bootleg, a set of tools and methods that we keep in our back pockets, and now you can do the same.'' German and Spanish versions are offered on the same page.', now(), 'Three notes, none disqualifying: 1. ONE FACTUAL ERROR IN THE PROPOSER''S DESCRIPTION. The card "Saturate and Group" does NOT exist in this 2018 edition — I grepped the full 90-page extracted text and there is no such card (that name is from the older 2010 Bootcamp Bootleg). Its function is carried by "Story Share-and-Capture," which does the same job (headlines on post-its, grouped and re-grouped on a board to find themes). If the proposer''s prose is reused as the catalog blurb, that card name…', 'seed'),
    ('People-centred designing', 'The Open University', 'https://www.open.edu/openlearn/science-maths-technology/design-innovation/people-centred-designing/content-section-0', 'course', 'user-research', 'user research — turning three real user conversations into one prioritized change', 'design', '12 hours total — about 4 weeks at 3 hours a week', 'none', 'The other three resources are articles and a card deck; this is the one real course, with a sequence, exercises and an ending, for a student who does better with structure than with ''go read this and try it.'' It is written at Level 1 (introductory) for learners with no prior study, and its sections are the research skill itself rather than design theory: ''9 User research techniques'', ''10 User research techniques: observing users'', ''12 The voice of the customer'', plus ''4 Who are the users?''. It teaches the thing the other resources assume — that you go find out what people actually do instead of guessing — and the observation sections are a useful corrective, since what users say in an…', true, 'Fetched the page. It states ''12 hours study'', ''Level 1: Introductory'', ''Free statement of participation on completion'', and — critically for a student with no credit card — ''You can start this course right now without signing-up.'' Signing up is optional and only adds progress tracking and the certificate; both are still free.', now(), 'Approved on the hard requirements, which it passes cleanly. But the skill fit is PARTIAL, and a curator should place it knowing exactly which half it covers. 1. IT TEACHES THE GATHERING HALF, NOT THE SYNTHESIS HALF. The assigned skill is "turning three real user conversations into one prioritized change." This course teaches how to go find out what real people do; it does NOT teach how to synthesize findings or decide what to change first. I checked specifically for this: section 13 "Products…', 'seed'),
    ('Easy Checks — A First Review of Web Accessibility', 'W3C Web Accessibility Initiative', 'https://www.w3.org/WAI/test-evaluate/easy-checks/', 'problem-set', 'accessibility', 'accessibility — making a prototype usable by everyone it is for', 'design', 'one afternoon (2-3 hours), then ~30 minutes each time you re-check a prototype', 'none — you need a web page to look at, nothing more. No coding required.', 'START HERE, this week. Every other accessibility resource teaches theory first; this one hands you 13 concrete checks you run on a real page today — image alt text, page title, headings, color contrast, keyboard focus, zoom, form labels — each with instructions for doing it in a normal browser with no special software. For a design-engineer whose skill rung is ''making the prototype usable by everyone it is for'', this converts the abstract word ''accessibility'' into a checklist you can run against your own prototype in an afternoon and come back with a list of things that are actually broken. Run it on your own project, then on a site you use daily; the gap between what you assumed and what…', true, 'Published by the W3C Web Accessibility Initiative and served free on w3.org with no sign-in or payment. The page states its purpose plainly: "This page helps you start to assess the accessibility of a web page. With these simple checks, you can find out whether or not accessibility is addressed in even the most basic way." It also warns "These checks cover just a few accessibility issues and are designed to be quick and easy, rather than exhaustive." W3C publishes WAI material under permissive open licensing.', now(), 'Two minor accuracy notes for a curator, neither disqualifying: 1. The proposer''s claim says each check has "instructions for doing it in a normal browser with no special software." Mostly true, but slightly overstated for one check: the color-contrast page''s *accurate* method sends you to WAI''s Evaluation Tools List rather than giving a browser-only procedure. The *quick* grayscale method on that same page needs nothing but the provided bookmarklet, and the linked tools are themselves free, so…', 'seed'),
    ('Learn Accessibility', 'web.dev', 'https://web.dev/learn/accessibility', 'course', 'accessibility', 'accessibility — making a prototype usable by everyone it is for', 'design', '3-4 weeks at ~3h/week for the 20 modules; you can do the design-side modules (color and contrast, typography,…', 'basic HTML helps a lot; you do not need JavaScript for most modules. If you have never written HTML, do a few hours of free HTML basics first (freeCodeCamp or MDN) and come back.', 'This is the spine of the roadmap — the one resource that goes from ''what is digital accessibility and why does it matter'' all the way to manual testing and testing with assistive technology, in order, in one place. It is unusually well-suited to the design-engineer archetype because it does not treat accessibility as only a code problem: there are dedicated modules on color and contrast, typography, animation and motion, and ''Design and user experience'', alongside the ARIA/keyboard/forms modules. Pick it over a video course because it is text you can skim, search, and return to as a reference when you are mid-prototype at 11pm. Suggested path after Easy Checks: modules 1-3 (what/why/how…', true, 'The full course text loads with no paywall, no trial, and no sign-in — the page describes itself as "An evergreen accessibility course and reference to level up your web development." One honest caveat: the optional end-of-module quizzes exist for collecting a badge, and badge collection may require a Google sign-in; the learning content itself does not. Skipping the badge costs you nothing.', now(), 'Three things a curator should know, none disqualifying. 1. The proposer''s badge caveat is more pessimistic than what I found. They warned that "badge collection may require a Google sign-in." I found no sign-in requirement on any of the six pages, and the Welcome page mentions no badges at all — only "a short assessment" per section. Either way the learning content is ungated, so the caveat is harmless, but do not let it propagate into student-facing copy as if part of the course is locked.…', 'seed'),
    ('Accessibility module (Core learning path) on MDN Web Docs', 'Mozilla / MDN Web Docs', 'https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development/Core/Accessibility', 'interactive', 'accessibility', 'accessibility — making a prototype usable by everyone it is for', 'design', '2-3 weeks at ~3h/week, including the ''Test your skills'' exercises and the troubleshooting challenge', 'stated by the page itself: "You should be familiar with HTML, CSS, and JavaScript before starting this module." Do this one after web.dev, not before.', 'This is the hands-on rung. Where web.dev explains, MDN makes you fix things: there are ''Test your skills'' exercises between the tutorials and a final ''Accessibility troubleshooting'' challenge where you are handed a deliberately broken sample site and have to diagnose and repair it. That is the closest free thing to the actual job of making a prototype usable. It also covers two areas the web.dev course treats more lightly and that matter for a student building a real app: WAI-ARIA basics for custom controls you built yourself, and mobile accessibility on iOS and Android. MDN is also the reference you will keep using for the next decade, so time spent learning to navigate it compounds.', true, 'MDN Web Docs is free and open, maintained by Mozilla and the developer community; the full module and every lesson page render without an account or payment. No pricing, trial, or registration prompt appears anywhere in the module.', now(), 'Three things a curator should know, none disqualifying. 1. Real prerequisite gate. The module states outright that the student should already be familiar with HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. This is not an accessibility-knowledge prerequisite (fine per the bar) but a coding one, and it is a harder entry point than a conceptual explainer. For Northstar''s purpose this is defensible — the assigned skill is "making a PROTOTYPE usable," so the student is assumed to be building something — but it should…', 'seed'),
    ('WebAIM WCAG 2 Checklist (plus the free Contrast Checker and WAVE tool)', 'WebAIM, Utah State University', 'https://webaim.org/standards/wcag/checklist', 'documentation', 'accessibility', 'accessibility — making a prototype usable by everyone it is for', 'design', '30 minutes to read through; then it is a permanent reference you use for an hour before every project ships', 'none to read; far more useful after Easy Checks and a few web.dev modules', 'The official WCAG standard is written in legal-grade language that is genuinely hard for an adult professional, let alone a 9th grader. This is the same requirements rewritten in plain English by a university accessibility center, which is why it is the version practitioners actually keep open. Keep it as your pre-submission checklist: before you show a prototype to a judge, a teacher, or a Congressional App Challenge reviewer, walk the list. The same site gives you two free tools you will use constantly — the Color Contrast Checker (paste two hex codes, get pass/fail) and WAVE, which scans a URL and marks problems visually. It is hosted by a university center rather than a company or an…', true, 'Free on webaim.org with no paywall or account; WebAIM is part of Utah State University. The page notes "A PDF version of this checklist is also available" at no cost, and WebAIM''s own sidebar lists "Free resources like WebAIM articles, resources, email discussion list, monthly newsletter, and blog," plus the Color Contrast Checker and the WAVE Web Accessibility Evaluation Tool. The page is explicit about what it is: "It is a checklist that presents our recommendations for implementing the most common accessibility principles and techniques for those seeking WCAG conformance."', now(), 'Three things a curator should know. None of them sink it, but the first two should shape how it is presented. 1. It is reference-shaped, not tutorial-shaped. This is a checklist, and the proposal is honest about that ("documentation", "keep it as your pre-submission checklist"). But it does not teach concepts from zero — it states requirements. Items assume the reader already knows what an image map hot spot, a contrast ratio, keyboard focus, an accessible name, and ARIA are. It links out to…', 'seed'),
    ('Book of Proof (3rd edition) — Chapter 6 "Proof by Contradiction" and Chapter 10 "Mathematical Induction"', 'Richard Hammack, Virginia Commonwealth University (official author page, PDF bui', 'https://richardhammack.github.io/BookOfProof/', 'textbook', 'proof-technique', 'proof technique — induction and contradiction until they are reflexes', 'math', 'Start week 1: read Ch. 4-6 (direct proof, contrapositive, contradiction), then Ch. 10 (induction, strong induction,…', 'None beyond high-school algebra and comfort with integers, evens/odds, and divisibility. The book builds sets, logic and quantifiers from scratch in Chapters 1-2 before any proof is asked for.', 'This is the single best starting point for this exact skill: the whole book is about nothing but how to write proofs, it is built for students who have never written one, and it has a full solutions section (starting p. 292 of the PDF) so a student with no teacher can self-check — which is what turns technique into reflex. Contradiction and induction are whole chapters, not subsections: Ch. 6 (6.1 Proving Statements with Contradiction, 6.2 Proving Conditional Statements by Contradiction, 6.3 Combining Techniques) and Ch. 10 (10.1 Proof by Induction, 10.2 Strong Induction, 10.3 Smallest Counterexample). No calculus, no programming, no account, no email address — a 9th grader can download…', true, 'The author''s page states: "You can also download a free PDF version HERE" (link to Main.pdf, which I downloaded successfully — 1.8 MB, LaTeX-produced, creation date 2025-02-05). The PDF''s own preface text reads "The book can be downloaded from [the] web page in PDF format for free, and the print version costs considerably less than comparable traditional textbooks." Licensed Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivative 4.0. Paper copies cost money ($28.18 paperback); the PDF that contains the identical content does not.', now(), 'Approve, but fix the catalog framing on one point — it matters for a student with no teacher. 1. A STUDENT CANNOT OPEN TO CH. 6 AND START. The proposer presents Ch. 6 and Ch. 10 as the entry point. The book''s own dependency tree (PDF idx 9) places Ch. 6 downstream of Ch. 1 (Sets), Ch. 2 (Logic), Ch. 4 (Direct Proof) and Ch. 5 (Contrapositive Proof) — contradiction starts on p. 137, i.e. ~137 pages in. The honest instruction is "work Ch. 1-2, then 4-6, then 10," not "read Chapters 6 and 10."…', 'seed'),
    ('Mathematical Reasoning: Writing and Proof, Version 3 — Chapter 3 (proof methods, incl. contradiction) and Chapter 4 (Mathematical Induction)', 'Ted Sundstrom, Grand Valley State University; published by GVSU Libraries via Sc', 'https://scholarworks.gvsu.edu/books/24/', 'textbook', 'proof-technique', 'proof technique — induction and contradiction until they are reflexes', 'math', '4-6 weeks at ~3 h/week for Ch. 3 and Ch. 4, doing every Preview Activity and Progress Check. Each section is one…', 'None beyond high-school algebra. The book is written as the first proof course in a college sequence and assumes no prior proof experience; Chapters 1-2 cover statements, logic and quantifiers first.', 'Use this as the practice partner to Hammack, or instead of it if a student learns better by doing than by reading. Its structure is unusually good for a student working alone: every section opens with "Preview Activities" meant to be done before reading, and "Progress Checks" stop you mid-section to make you actually write something — with Appendix B giving answers to every Progress Check and Appendix C giving answers and hints to selected exercises. There are also free screencasts on YouTube made by the author to accompany the book, so a stuck student has a voice explaining the section, not just a page. Chapter 4 is entirely Mathematical Induction (including strong induction and…', true, 'The ScholarWorks@GVSU page offers the full text (6.1 MB) and individual chapters as free downloads and states the work is licensed under a "Creative Commons Attribution-Noncommercial-Share Alike 3.0 License." No account, payment, or email is requested. (Note: the direct cgi/viewcontent PDF link returns 403 to automated fetchers; the landing page linked here serves the download normally in a browser. A mirror of the same book, same CC BY-NC-SA license, is also readable as web pages at math.libretexts.org if the download ever misbehaves.)', now(), 'Approve, but fix three overstatements in the proposer''s blurb before it reaches students: 1. "Preview Activities" is stale terminology for Version 3 — this is the one substantive wording error. The page states: "the preview activities in Version 2.1 have been renamed to beginning activities in Version 3. This was done to emphasize that these activities are meant to be completed before starting the rest of the section and are not just a short preview of what is to come." The feature exists…', 'seed'),
    ('MIT 6.042J Mathematics for Computer Science (Spring 2015) — Unit 1: Proofs (video lectures, problem sets with solutions, free textbook)', 'MIT OpenCourseWare / MIT Open Learning Library', 'https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-042j-mathematics-for-computer-science-spring-2015/', 'video-series', 'proof-technique', 'proof technique — induction and contradiction until they are reflexes', 'math', 'Unit 1 only (Sessions 1-11): about 6-8 weeks at 3 h/week — watch the lecture, then do the problem set and grade…', 'Algebra and comfort with summation notation. Caution, stated honestly: MIT''s own course description lists "familiarity with sequences and series, limits, and differentiation and integration of functions of one variable" as necessary — that applies to the later units (counting, asymptotics,…', 'Hammack and Sundstrom teach you to read proofs; this makes you watch a mathematician build one live and then attempt graded-style problems with published solutions — the closest a student with no teacher gets to being corrected. Unit 1 covers exactly this skill in order: proof methods and proof by contradiction (Sessions 1-4), the Well Ordering Principle (Sessions 4-5), and Induction and strong induction (Sessions 6-7). It also carries the strongest name recognition of anything on this list, which matters for a student who needs to tell an application "I taught myself proof-writing from MIT''s discrete math course" — and unlike a certificate course, the transcript-worthy part (the problem…', true, 'The course page states the course is "part of the Open Learning Library, which is free to use. You have the option to sign up and enroll in the course if you want to track your progress, or you can view and use all the materials without enrolling," and the site is Creative Commons licensed. The readings page states "The full course textbook is Mathematics for Computer Science. Individual chapters are available for download below," each as a free PDF. Lecture videos, lecture notes, problem sets, exams and the textbook are all listed as included materials. Nothing is gated behind payment or a…', now(), 'The RESOURCE passes all five hard requirements. But the proposer''s blurb contains two factual errors that must be fixed before this ships, or it becomes another inaccurate row. 1. FALSE — "problem sets with solutions" (in the title) and "attempt graded-style problems with published solutions." The course explicitly refuses this: "Solutions to the following Problem Sets are not available to the general public." Strike "with solutions" from the title and rewrite the claim. The defensible…', 'seed'),
    ('Discrete Mathematics: An Open Introduction (3rd ed.) — §2.5 Induction and §3.2 Proof by Contradiction', 'Oscar Levin, School of Mathematical Sciences, University of Northern Colorado', 'https://discrete.openmathbooks.org/dmoi3/sec_seq-induction.html', 'interactive', 'proof-technique', 'proof technique — induction and contradiction until they are reflexes', 'math', 'One weekend for the induction section end to end. The section has 29 exercises with hints and solutions attached, many…', 'None beyond high-school algebra and exponent rules. Chapter 0 builds sets, functions and mathematical statements first; the induction section itself opens by placing induction alongside "direct proof or proof by contradiction," so reading §3.2 or a proof-methods chapter first helps but is not…', 'This is the drill resource. It reads in a browser on a phone with no download, and the linked section is the induction section itself, so a student lands on the material rather than a homepage. Its examples are deliberately concrete and non-intimidating — making any postage over 28 cents from 5- and 8-cent stamps, breaking a chocolate bar into n squares, proving n² < 2ⁿ for n ≥ 5, proving every integer above 1 factors into primes — each written out with the base case and inductive step labeled, which is precisely the ritual a beginner needs to copy until it is reflex. §3.2 covers proof by contradiction the same way. Use it after reading Hammack''s chapter, for volume of practice with…', true, 'I fetched the full induction section and it rendered complete — all six worked examples and all 29 exercises with hints and "Selected Solutions" visible, nothing truncated, no login, no paywall, no email wall. The book''s front matter describes it as an open-source textbook published with PreTeXt and hosted on Runestone Academy and confirms free online availability, and it is listed on the American Institute of Mathematics'' approved open-textbook list. I am unsure of the exact license string (the front matter fetch did not surface a verbatim CC line), so I am claiming free access, which I…', now(), 'Two metadata errors a curator must fix before this row ships. Neither affects access or accuracy of the learning material. 1. kind is wrong. Proposed as "interactive" and the claim says "PreTeXt/Runestone." I checked: the dmoi3 pages are static PreTeXt HTML with expandable/revealable "Solution" text, plus navigation and search. There are no Runestone dynamic activities — no clickable problem-solving tools, no auto-graded input, no progress tracking. It reads well on a phone with no download,…', 'seed'),
    ('OpenStax College Physics 2e, §1.3: Accuracy, Precision, and Significant Figures', 'OpenStax', 'https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/1-3-accuracy-precision-and-significant-figures', 'textbook', 'error-analysis', 'error analysis — knowing how much to trust every number you record', 'physics', 'one evening, about 2 hours', 'none — arithmetic, percentages, and a calculator', 'START HERE. This is the only resource on the list that assumes literally nothing — a 9th grader with percentages and a calculator can read it cover to cover tonight. It separates the two words students use interchangeably (accuracy = how close to true; precision = how tightly clustered), defines uncertainty as the +/- attached to every number, teaches percent uncertainty with a fully worked example (Example 1.2, weighing a bag of apples), gives the two propagation rules for multiply/divide and add/subtract, and connects significant figures to uncertainty rather than treating them as an arbitrary rule. There are ''Check Your Understanding'' problems with solutions shown inline, so a student…', true, 'The page itself carries the line: "Access for free at https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/1-introduction-to-science-and-the-realm-of-physics-physical-quantities-and-units", and the section is published under a Creative Commons license. The full text renders in the browser with no account, no email, and no trial. OpenStax is a Rice University nonprofit whose entire model is free textbooks.', now(), 'Two caveats, neither disqualifying: 1. SCOPE IS NARROWER THAN THE SKILL NAME. This section teaches measurement uncertainty and significant figures. It does NOT cover random vs. systematic error as a conceptual distinction, standard deviation or standard error, error bars, or residuals/goodness-of-fit. The skill is "error analysis — knowing how much to trust every number you record," and this covers roughly the first third of that: the uncertainty attached to a single instrument reading. The…', 'seed'),
    ('A Beginner''s Guide to Uncertainty of Measurement (NPL Good Practice Guide No. 11)', 'National Physical Laboratory', 'https://eprintspublications.npl.co.uk/1568/1/MGPG11.pdf', 'pdf', 'error-analysis', 'error analysis — knowing how much to trust every number you record', 'physics', 'a weekend, roughly 4-5 hours for the 33 pages plus the worked example', 'none — the guide builds mean and standard deviation from scratch in section 3. A calculator or free spreadsheet helps. Best read after the OpenStax section so the word ''uncertainty'' is already familiar.', 'This is the resource that actually answers the skill as it is worded — how much to trust a number — rather than just teaching arithmetic rules. It is written by a metrologist at the UK''s national measurement lab, the people whose job is deciding what a kilogram is, and it builds everything from zero: section 3 derives mean and standard deviation from a set of readings without assuming any statistics; section 4 catalogues where errors actually come from (the instrument, the thing being measured, the room, the operator); section 5 explains random vs systematic and why the shape of the error distribution matters; section 7 introduces the professional vocabulary a student will meet in every…', true, 'NPL''s own resource page describes it as "a gentle and short introduction to uncertainty of measurement for beginners, including laboratories preparing for UKAS accreditation" and offers it as a free PDF download with no fee or registration mentioned. The PDF itself, served from NPL''s own eprints repository with no login, reads on its title page: "Measurement Good Practice Guide No. 11 (Issue 2) — A Beginner''s Guide to Uncertainty of Measurement, Stephanie Bell, Centre for Basic, Thermal and Length Metrology, National Physical Laboratory ... (c) Crown Copyright 1999. Reproduced by permission…', now(), 'Approving, but four things a curator should know. None is disqualifying; the first is the one I would actually surface. 1. IT IS 25 YEARS OLD, AND SOME OF ITS ONWARD POINTERS ARE STALE. Published August 1999, "Issue 2 with amendments March 2001." The core content is not affected — mean, standard deviation, Type A/B evaluation and quadrature are GUM fundamentals that have not changed — so the teaching is still correct. But Section 16 "Further reading" points at specific editions of UKAS M3003…', 'seed'),
    ('Lab Guide 1: Introduction to Error and Uncertainty (Columbia University Physics)', 'Columbia University, Department of Physics', 'https://www.physics.columbia.edu/sites/default/files/content/Lab%20Resources/Lab%20Guide%201_%20Introduction%20to%20Error%20and%20Uncertainty.pdf', 'problem-set', 'error-analysis', 'error analysis — knowing how much to trust every number you record', 'physics', 'one to two afternoons, about 3 hours for the 16 questions', 'Algebra 1 and the propagation rules from the NPL guide or the LibreTexts page. A free spreadsheet (Google Sheets or LibreOffice Calc) substitutes for the Excel the guide assumes.', 'This is the proof-of-mastery step, and it is the piece students almost never find on their own: 16 numbered practice questions at the end of the guide, covering exactly this skill and nothing else. Verified in the PDF text: estimating uncertainty from a distribution of measured values, the absolute and relative uncertainty of a radioactive count (16 counts vs 1600 counts, and how many counts you need for 1% relative uncertainty — the Poisson intuition, taught without the word), significant digits in a stated uncertainty, propagating uncertainty through the pendulum formula to get g, propagating through a box volume from three measured sides, a seven-part drill on combining A +/- dA with B…', true, 'The 16-page PDF is served directly from physics.columbia.edu with no login, no paywall, and no registration — I fetched it and extracted the text to confirm it is the document claimed. Page 1 footer reads "(c) GNU General Public License", an explicitly free license. Header quote: "Learning Goals: Understand how to measure error in a lab experiment; Study how to propagate error from an initial measurement through a calculation; Understand how uncertainty is an integral part of any lab experiment."', now(), 'Three corrections to the proposing agent''s write-up. A curator should fix the first before publishing. 1. THE LICENSE CLAIM IS FABRICATED. The proposal states: ''Page 1 footer reads "(c) GNU General Public License", an explicitly free license.'' That string does not exist. Zero hits for GNU, "General Public", copyright, ©, license, or "Creative Commons" in the full extracted text of all 16 pages AND in `strings` over the raw PDF binary. Page 1 ends with the 2.5 vs 1.5 worked example and a bare…', 'seed'),
    ('CTRL ALT FTC — Introduction to Closed Loop Control + The PID Controller', 'CTRL ALT FTC (Ben Caunt / FTC Team 22377 The Sigmacorns), open', 'https://www.ctrlaltftc.com/introduction-to-closed-loop-control', 'textbook', 'control-basics', 'control basics — why the naive approach oscillates and PID does not', 'engineering', 'one weekend to read the closed-loop + P/I/D chapters; a second weekend to code and tune one mechanism', 'Basic programming (Java or any language with loops and variables). No calculus, no physics course.', 'This is the only resource found that opens on exactly the assigned question. Its closed-loop chapter introduces bang-bang control (the naive approach: full power until you pass the target, then full power back), says it ''can lead to undesirable oscillations,'' and then shows the proportional controller as the smooth, continuous fix — with separate short chapters for the Proportional, Integral and Derivative terms (P = rubber-band stiffness, I = overcomes friction/steady-state error, D = damping). Written for high school FTC robotics programmers with Java pseudocode you can paste into a real robot, so no calculus is needed. Honest caveat: it is pragmatic rather than rigorous — the page…', true, 'Site loads in full with no signup, paywall or account prompt; full text is also published openly at https://www.ctrlaltftc.com/llms-full.txt and the project is open source on GitHub. Page states it is a guide ''for those with a background in FTC programming.''', now(), 'Two honest caveats a curator should carry forward; neither is disqualifying, but both should shape how the row is written. 1. STATED PREREQUISITE IS NARROWER THAN THE PROPOSAL IMPLIED. The homepage says verbatim: "This guide assumes that you have experience with the SDK," and frames itself as "for those who have just learned the basics of programming but are stuck in the grey ''middle area'' where resources are far below your abilities or just a little above your head." So the intended reader…', 'seed'),
    ('WPILib Advanced Controls — Introduction to PID and Tuning a PID Controller', 'Worcester Polytechnic Institute / FIRST Robotics Competition', 'https://docs.wpilib.org/en/stable/docs/software/advanced-controls/introduction/introduction-to-pid.html', 'documentation', 'control-basics', 'control basics — why the naive approach oscillates and PID does not', 'engineering', '3-5 hours to read the Advanced Controls introduction section; a weekend if you also play with the tuning simulators', 'The prose is readable with algebra, but the equations use derivative and integral notation — a student who has not seen calculus should read the CTRL ALT FTC chapters first, then come here. Code examples assume Java, C++ or Python.', 'The most durable and robotics-native option: official FRC control documentation maintained by WPI, versioned per season, edited in the open on GitHub. It names the failure modes of the naive approach in the right vocabulary — ''An underdamped response oscillates around the reference before settling,'' and for proportional-only control, ''the proportional term may be too small to pull the output all the way to the setpoint, and the derivative term is zero. This can result in steady-state error'' — which is precisely the argument for why P alone is not enough and why I and D exist. The companion tuning pages (e.g. /introduction/tuning-flywheel.html) embed interactive in-browser simulators, so a…', true, 'Page loads publicly with no login, paywall, or price anywhere on it; it is open-source documentation with ''Edit on GitHub'' links on every page.', now(), 'Three notes for the curator, none disqualifying. 1. HOST FIELD IS WRONG AS PROPOSED — please fix before ingest. The proposer lists the host as "Worcester Polytechnic Institute / FIRST Robotics Competition." The page''s own copyright line reads "© Copyright 2026, FIRST and other WPILib Contributors," and the GitHub links point to the wpilibsuite org, not to WPI. WPI is the origin of the name (WPI Library) and hosts a sibling "PID Introduction Video by WPI," so the association is real, but the…', 'seed'),
    ('Understanding PID Control (MATLAB Tech Talks, 7-part video series)', 'MathWorks', 'https://www.mathworks.com/videos/series/understanding-pid-control.html', 'video-series', 'control-basics', 'control basics — why the naive approach oscillates and PID does not', 'engineering', 'about 90 minutes of video; one evening straight through, or ~3 hours with notes', 'None. High school algebra is enough to follow; no programming and no MATLAB needed to watch.', 'The best intuition-builder for a student who wants the ''why'' without the math wall. Part 1 (''What Is PID Control?'', 11:41) states in its own description that it ''skips most of the math and instead focuses on building a solid foundation'' — it explains what a controller is for and why naive feedback oscillates before introducing each term. The series then covers exactly the traps a first-time robot builder hits: anti-windup (10:43), why a raw derivative amplifies sensor noise (11:52), a tuning guide (12:04), building a model (13:44), manual vs automatic tuning (13:30), and important concepts like cascaded and discrete loops (12:28). Corporate-hosted but foundation-grade durable — these Tech…', true, 'The series page lists all seven videos with durations and carries no paywall, trial offer, or sign-in requirement for viewing; the only account links (''Get MATLAB'', ''Sign In'') are site-wide header navigation, not gates on the videos. Honest caveat: watching is free, but reproducing the Simulink demos shown on screen would require MATLAB, which is paid — do not plan on that part.', now(), 'THE PAID-SOFTWARE CAVEAT MUST SURVIVE INTO THE CATALOG BLURB. The proposer was honest about this and the honesty is load-bearing: watching all seven videos costs nothing, but the on-screen demos are Simulink, and MATLAB/Simulink is expensive. A student with $0 gets the full conceptual education and none of the hands-on. If the blurb is trimmed to "free PID video series" and the caveat is dropped, a freshman will open it expecting to follow along and hit a wall. Keep a sentence like "watch for…', 'seed'),
    ('PID Without a PhD (Tim Wescott)', 'Carnegie Mellon University Qatar, course 16', 'https://web2.qatar.cmu.edu/~gdicaro/16311-Fall17/slides/PID-without-PhD.pdf', 'pdf', 'control-basics', 'control basics — why the naive approach oscillates and PID does not', 'engineering', 'about 2 hours to read; a weekend to implement the C loop and run the tuning procedure on a motor or a simulation', 'Able to read C-style code and comfortable with high school algebra. The author explicitly assumes no control theory.', 'The classic practical answer to ''why does my controller oscillate.'' It blames the real culprit — lag — rather than hand-waving: with the motor example, raising proportional gain makes the response faster but overshoot grows because ''the motor position doesn''t start ramping up immediately. This delay, plus high feedback gain, is what causes the overshoot,'' illustrated with step-response figures at increasing gains. Sections: Control Loops, PID Control, Example Plants, Controllers, Tuning, Other Issues. The Tuning section gives a concrete recipe (adjust derivative, then proportional, then integral, moving gains by factors of 2, 8 or 10) that a student can actually follow without a model of…', true, 'The CMU-hosted PDF downloads in full (1.7 MB) and its extracted text is the complete article, beginning ''PID Without a PhD / Tim Wescott, Wescott Design Services / I both consult and teach in the area of digital control...''. The HTML version at embeddedrelated.com renders the entire article with no login or payment.', now(), 'Four things a curator should know, none disqualifying: 1. LINK-ROT RISK ON THE PROPOSED URL — the real concern here. The path is a tilde personal faculty directory for a specific dead semester: ~gdicaro/16311-Fall17/. That is Prof. Gianni Di Caro''s personal space for a Fall 2017 course, now nine years old. It is a cmu.edu host and it loads today, but course-archive paths inside personal directories are exactly the kind of URL that disappears when a professor moves institutions or IT prunes old…', 'seed'),
    ('The Device Development Process (FDA, Learn About Drug and Device Approvals)', 'U.S. Food and Drug Administration', 'https://www.fda.gov/patients/learn-about-drug-and-device-approvals/device-development-process', 'documentation', 'regulatory-literacy', 'regulatory literacy — why medical ideas move slowly and what that means for design', 'health', 'one afternoon (1-2 hours)', 'none', 'This is the only beginner-level, plain-English map of the whole road that a 9th grader can read cold with no prior coursework. It lays out the five stages a device actually passes through — Device Discovery and Concept, Preclinical Research/Prototype, Pathway to Approval, FDA Review, Post-Market Safety Monitoring — in language written for patients, not for regulatory professionals (verified sentences include ''Devices undergo laboratory and animal testing to answer basic questions about safety''). Start here before anything else: every other resource on this list assumes you already know that ''Pathway to Approval'' branches into 510(k) vs. PMA vs. De Novo, and this is the page that tells you…', true, 'Fetched and it loads as a normal public FDA.gov page under fda.gov/patients — no paywall, no login, no registration prompt, no ''create an account'' step anywhere on the page. It is a U.S. federal government publication, which is public domain.', now(), 'Approve the resource, but the proposer''s description needs two corrections before it goes in front of a student — both are the same species of hand-authored inaccuracy the audit flagged. 1. FIX THE 510(k)/PMA/De Novo CLAIM. As written, the description promises something the linked page does not deliver. A student who clicks expecting the three pathways gets ~400 words and no mention of them. The fix is one sentence: say this URL is the table of contents for a five-part series and that the…', 'seed'),
    ('CDRH Learn — FDA''s free medical device regulation training modules', 'FDA Center for Devices and Radiological Health (CDRH), Office of Training and Ed', 'https://www.fda.gov/training-and-continuing-education/cdrh-learn', 'video-series', 'regulatory-literacy', 'regulatory literacy — why medical ideas move slowly and what that means for design', 'health', '4 weeks at 3h/week if you work through ''Start Here/The Basics'' plus ''How to Study and Market Your Device''', 'Read the FDA Device Development Process page first so the acronyms (510(k), PMA, IDE) already mean something', 'This is the closest thing to an actual free course on device regulation, taught by the regulator itself — the people who decide. Verified live: the page organizes modules into ''Start Here/The Basics'' (device classification, MDUFA), ''How to Study and Market Your Device'' (510(k), De Novo, IDE, PMA, HDE, Q-Submissions, Standards), ''Postmarket Activities'' (recalls, Medical Device Reporting, inspections), ''In Vitro Diagnostics'', ''UDI'', and ''Specialty Technical Topics'' (digital health, biocompatibility, human factors). Each module comes as video plus printable slides plus a transcript, so it works on a phone and works offline if your internet is bad. Honest caveat: it is written for industry,…', true, 'Fetched the page. No payment, subscription, login, or registration requirement appears anywhere; presentation links, printable slides, and transcripts are directly accessible. FDA''s own program description calls it ''a multi-media educational resource, featuring learning modules'' provided to the public, and it is a U.S. government publication.', now(), 'Three caveats a curator should carry forward, none disqualifying: 1. AUDIENCE IS INDUSTRY, AND THE PAGE SAYS SO. Verbatim from the page: "These modules provide industry with information that is comprehensive, interactive, and easily accessible." The proposer disclosed this honestly and their mitigation is the right one — do "Start Here/The Basics" first, then only two or three pathway modules. Do NOT present this to students as a course to complete. Mitigating factor I confirmed: the authoring…', 'seed'),
    ('Applying Human Factors and Usability Engineering to Medical Devices (FDA final guidance)', 'FDA Center for Devices and Radiological Health', 'https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-information/search-fda-guidance-documents/applying-human-factors-and-usability-engineering-medical-devices', 'pdf', 'regulatory-literacy', 'regulatory literacy — why medical ideas move slowly and what that means for design', 'health', 'two weekends (5-7 hours), reading with a notebook', 'none for the human-factors sections, though the Device Development Process page makes the regulatory vocabulary easier', 'This is the ''what that means for design'' half of the skill, and it is the one document that turns regulation from a bureaucratic obstacle into a design brief. It is FDA''s final guidance on designing for real users in real environments: use-related risk analysis, foreseeable misuse, intended-user populations, use environments, and human factors validation testing. A student who reads it stops designing for a demo and starts designing for a tired nurse at 3 a.m. — which is exactly the difference between a science-fair prototype and a device. The page confirms the document is Final (not draft) and gives a direct PDF download; I fetched the PDF itself (1.4 MB) to confirm it is the real…', true, 'The guidance page offers ''Download the Final Guidance Document'' as a direct PDF link (fda.gov/media/80481/download) with no account, email, or payment step; I successfully downloaded the full 1.4 MB PDF without any credentials. FDA guidance documents are U.S. government publications, free to the public by law.', now(), 'Approved, but five things a curator should fix or know: 1. IT COVERS ONE HALF OF THE SKILL, NOT BOTH. The skill is "why medical ideas move slowly AND what that means for design." This document is excellent, direct instruction on the design half. On the "why slowly" half it is only implicit — a student infers the answer from the evidence burden (risk analysis, formative evaluations, validation testing with separate participant groups per user population, documentation for marketing…', 'seed'),
    ('Medical Devices and the Public''s Health: The FDA 510(k) Clearance Process at 35 Years', 'National Academies of Sciences, Engineering, and Medicine (Institute of Medicine', 'https://www.nationalacademies.org/read/13150/chapter/1', 'textbook', 'regulatory-literacy', 'regulatory literacy — why medical ideas move slowly and what that means for design', 'health', 'a weekend for the Summary chapter (~2 hours); ~10 hours for the full report', 'Know what a 510(k) is — get that from the FDA Device Development Process page or CDRH Learn''s ''The Basics'' first', 'Every other resource here tells you what the rules are. This one tells you why the rules are fought over — and that is the actual skill, because a designer who understands the argument can make a regulatory choice on purpose instead of by accident. It is the expert committee report that examined whether the 510(k) ''substantially equivalent to an existing device'' pathway really establishes safety and effectiveness, and it is the single best explanation of the tradeoff at the heart of medical device regulation: clear devices faster and risk harm, or demand more evidence and delay a device patients need. Read the Summary chapter only — it is written for policymakers and the general reader,…', true, 'The report''s read-online page lists ''PDF FREE Download'' as a format option alongside the paid Paperback ($65.00) and Ebook ($54.99), and the complete text is readable in the browser chapter by chapter at no cost. Verified by fetching the reading interface directly.', now(), '1. THE URL IS OFF BY ONE CHAPTER — please fix before shipping. The claim tells the student "Read the Summary chapter only," but /chapter/1 is Front Matter (title page, committee roster, reviewers, preface). The Summary is /chapter/2, which I fetched and verified. A 9th grader clicking this link lands on a list of committee members'' names and university affiliations and may reasonably think the link is broken or that the report is beyond them. Change the stored URL to…', 'seed'),
    ('Calling Bullshit: Data Reasoning in a Digital World (INFO 270 / BIOL 270)', 'University of Washington', 'https://www.callingbullshit.org/syllabus.html', 'course', 'statistical-honesty', 'statistical honesty — confounders, base rates, and when a trend is noise', 'humanities', '4 weeks at 3h/week to watch the 10 lectures and work 3-4 case studies; the causality and statistical-traps weeks alone…', 'none — no statistics, no calculus; comfort reading news articles and reading a graph', 'This is the assigned skill, taught as an entire course, and it is the single best starting point for a data-journalist archetype because every example is a real news claim, chart, or headline rather than a textbook exercise. Week 4 is causality (correlation is not causation, common causes, spurious correlation, post hoc reasoning), Week 5 is the statistical traps — the base-rate fallacy and the prosecutor''s fallacy by name — and the case studies page teaches ''is this trend noise?'' directly through worked examples like the track-and-field records study, which the site describes as showing ''how differences in sample sizes can create misleading patterns,'' and the musician-mortality graphic…', true, 'Fetched the syllabus, videos, and case-studies pages: all load in full with no paywall, no account, and no purchase step. Syllabus states ''For each week, a set of required readings are assigned. Supplementary readings are also provided for those who wish to delve deeper,'' with the readings linked directly as public PDFs and articles. Videos page: ''The full playlist of all course videos is available on the UW Information School''s YouTube channel.'' Caveat to be honest about: the lecture videos are hosted on YouTube (UW iSchool''s official channel), while the syllabus, slides, case studies and…', now(), 'Four things a curator should know. None is disqualifying, but two should shape how this is presented. 1. NAME AND PROFANITY — the presentation issue, not a content issue. The resource is literally titled "Calling Bullshit" and the word appears in the course title, every week heading, the URL, and throughout the prose. The content is a for-credit University of Washington course and is analytically serious, but: (a) some K-12 school network filters block the domain on the string alone, so a…', 'seed'),
    ('OpenIntro Statistics (4th edition)', 'OpenIntro (501(c)(3) nonprofit)', 'https://www.openintro.org/book/os/', 'textbook', 'statistical-honesty', 'statistical honesty — confounders, base rates, and when a trend is noise', 'humanities', 'a weekend for Chapter 1 (study design and confounding); ~5 weeks at 3h/week to reach Chapter 5 and actually understand…', 'Algebra 1 (solving equations, reading a formula). No calculus.', 'Calling Bullshit teaches you to smell the problem; this teaches you the machinery so you can say why. Chapter 1 covers data-collection principles, observational studies vs. experiments, and confounding variables — the formal version of the confounder rung — and Chapter 5 builds sampling variability and the Central Limit Theorem, which is the honest answer to ''when is a trend just noise.'' It is the standard free stats textbook, used per the site ''at Community Colleges to the Ivy League,'' and unlike a video course it comes with exercises and solutions, so a student alone can check their own work. Choose this over MIT 18.05 (also free and excellent) because 18.05 assumes single-variable…', true, 'The download page lists ''FREE -- OpenIntro Statistics PDF'' and, on the optional-contribution step, ''If you want to skip the optional contribution, set the price to $0.'' Free video lectures, slides, labs (R/Python and others), and class activities are linked per chapter on the same page. The paid option is a printed copy only.', now(), 'Approve, but three small corrections a curator should make to the blurb before it ships: 1. LINK THE DIRECT PDF, NOT THE LEANPUB ONE. The headline "FREE PDF" button sends the student off openintro.org to Leanpub, where they must enter an email and set a price slider to $0. That is friction and it looks like a paywall to a 14-year-old who has been told this is free. The screen-reader PDF link on the same page is a direct openintro.org download — application/pdf, 200, no account, no email, no…', 'seed'),
    ('Statistics Done Wrong: The Woefully Complete Guide', 'Alex Reinhart, Department of Statistics & Data Science, Carnegie Mellon Universi', 'https://www.statisticsdonewrong.com/p-value.html', 'textbook', 'statistical-honesty', 'statistical honesty — confounders, base rates, and when a trend is noise', 'humanities', 'a weekend — roughly 5-6 hours to read the whole thing; the p-value/base-rate chapter is about 45 minutes', 'none strictly, but it is far more useful after you know roughly what a p-value and a control group are — i.e. after Calling Bullshit week 5 or OpenIntro chapter 5', 'This is the sharpest free treatment of the two hardest pieces of the skill: base rates and noise masquerading as a finding. The chapter ''The p value and the base rate fallacy'' works the medical-testing example that makes base rates click, and the book then covers statistical power and underpowered studies, regression to the mean and stopping rules (why a trend reverts), and researcher freedom / false discovery — exactly the failure modes a student will meet when they read a press release about a study. It is prose, not equations, so it lands well for a humanities student, and it is short enough to finish, which matters more than coverage for a first pass. Read it after you know what a…', true, 'Fetched the chapter page directly: the full chapter body is readable with no paywall and no signup. Sample body text present on the page: ''Because the base rate of effective cancer drugs is so low – only 10% of our hundred trial drugs actually work – most of the tested drugs do not work, and we have many opportunities for false positives.'' The site states the text is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International. A print/eBook edition is sold by No Starch Press, but the complete web text is free. Durability note: this is one academic''s site rather than a university server —…', now(), 'Three things a curator must fix or know before this row ships. None is a reason to reject, but the first is a factual correction. 1. THE PROPOSED `host` FIELD IS INACCURATE AS WRITTEN — CORRECT IT. It reads "Alex Reinhart, Department of Statistics & Data Science, Carnegie Mellon University," which reads as though CMU hosts this. CMU does not. It is a personal domain, statisticsdonewrong.com; the site root states no institutional affiliation at all, only a link to the author''s personal site.…', 'seed'),
    ('Seeing Theory: A Visual Introduction to Probability and Statistics', 'Brown University', 'https://seeing-theory.brown.edu/frequentist-inference/index.html', 'interactive', 'statistical-honesty', 'statistical honesty — confounders, base rates, and when a trend is noise', 'humanities', '2-3 hours in one sitting for the Frequentist Inference and Bayesian Inference chapters; a weekend for all six', 'Algebra 1 and some exposure to what an average and a percentage mean; the pages use a little notation, so it reads best alongside OpenIntro chapters 1-5 rather than cold', 'The one thing text cannot do is show you noise. Here you drag a slider, drop 100 or 1000 samples, and watch confidence intervals and sample estimates jump around on their own — which is the intuition behind ''that trend might be nothing.'' The Bayesian Inference chapter is the visual version of base rates. Use it as a 2-hour companion while reading OpenIntro chapter 5, not as a standalone course: it builds intuition and has no exercises.', true, 'Fetched the frequentist-inference page: interactive visualizations load (point estimation with ''Drop 100 Samples'' / ''Drop 1000 Samples'', interval estimation with adjustable sample size and confidence level, bootstrap resampling), nothing is gated, and a draft PDF textbook is downloadable free. Honest caveat: the site states it ''is archived for reference,'' so it is no longer updated — but it is still served and fully functional on Brown''s domain.', now(), 'One gap the proposer did not disclose, and a curator should know it: CONFOUNDERS ARE NOT TAUGHT ANYWHERE ON THIS SITE. I fetched the Regression Analysis chapter specifically to check. Its three sections are Ordinary Least Squares, Correlation, and Analysis of Variance, and it never mentions confounding variables, omitted variable bias, or correlation versus causation — it is pure methodology with no interpretive caution. So of the three things the assigned skill names, this resource covers…', 'seed'),
    ('Pixar in a Box: The Art of Storytelling — "Story structure" unit', 'Khan Academy (501(c)(3) nonprofit) in partnership with Pixar Animation Studios', 'https://www.khanacademy.org/humanities/hass-storytelling/storytelling-pixar-in-a-box', 'interactive', 'narrative-structure', 'narrative structure — hook, stakes, one idea, and nothing else', 'humanities', 'a weekend (3-5 hours for the story-structure and theme lessons; ~10 hours for the whole course)', 'none', 'This is the one resource on the list that assumes zero prior anything. It teaches the exact three moves in the skill: the "story spine" (Once upon a time... Every day... Until one day... = hook and turn), what raises stakes for a character, and "theme" — the single idea a story is actually about, which is the science-communicator''s ''one idea and nothing else.'' It is video plus written exercises, taught by Pixar directors, so a student practices on their own story instead of just reading advice. Start here, then apply the structure to science with the other three resources. Not science-specific, which is the point: learn the shape on something easy first.', true, 'Khan Academy is a nonprofit with no paid tier or certificate upsell; all course content is open. HONESTY NOTE — partial verification: WebFetch returned HTTP 200 for this URL and for the lesson URL .../ah-piab-story-structure/v/video1a-fine, confirming the pages are live, but Khan Academy renders content in JavaScript and blocked my headless browser with a bot challenge, so I could not read the rendered lesson list myself. Search listings independently show the unit "Story structure" at this exact path with videos titled "Story spine" and "Theme." I am confident the course is free and live; I…', now(), 'Approve, but fix three things before it ships. 1. THE PROPOSER''S VERIFICATION WAS NOT VALID, even though its conclusion was right. They wrote "WebFetch returned HTTP 200 for this URL... confirming the pages are live." It confirms no such thing: khanacademy.org serves its bot challenge WITH status 200. Every plain fetch I made — curl (desktop UA), curl (Googlebot UA), WebFetch, even /robots.txt and /sitemap.xml — returned the identical 3,038-byte page titled "Client Challenge". Any Khan Academy…', 'seed'),
    ('Storygrams — annotated science stories', 'The Open Notebook (science', 'https://www.theopennotebook.com/category/storygrams/', 'documentation', 'narrative-structure', 'narrative structure — hook, stakes, one idea, and nothing else', 'humanities', '4 weeks at 1-2h/week (read and mark up one Storygram per week)', 'none — able to read a long magazine article', 'Structure advice is abstract until you watch it happen. A Storygram is a real published science story with a working science journalist''s annotations laid over it, pointing at exactly where the hook lands, where the stakes get named, what the single idea is, and what the writer cut. It is the closest thing to a free apprenticeship in this skill, and it is the only resource here where a student sees professional-grade science writing taken apart paragraph by paragraph. Have the student read the story once clean, guess the structure, then read the annotations.', true, 'Fetched the category page: it lists annotated stories (e.g. "North Atlantic Right Whales Are Dying in Horrific Ways" by Ed Yong, "Accidental Therapists" by Eric Boodman) with no paywall, no login, and no subscription prompt. The About page states the organization''s aim that "resources needed to cover science can be provided to any journalist, anywhere in the world, for free."', now(), 'Two honest caveats, neither disqualifying. 1. The proposing agent''s suggested workflow — "read the story once clean, guess the structure, then read the annotations" — only works on some Storygrams. The Oaster entry reproduces the full story text on the page, so it works there. The Ed Yong entry does NOT reproduce the full story; it quotes excerpts inline with annotations and links out to The Atlantic, which runs a metered paywall. A student with $0 may hit that meter. This does not break the…', 'seed'),
    ('The Message Box Workbook', 'COMPASS Science Communication, Inc. (501(c)(3) nonprofit)', 'https://www.compassscicomm.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/05/The-Message-Box-Workbook.pdf', 'pdf', 'narrative-structure', 'narrative structure — hook, stakes, one idea, and nothing else', 'humanities', 'one afternoon (~2-3 hours), then 30 min each time you have something new to explain', 'none — helps to have one science topic you already care about to run through the boxes', 'This is the drill for the hardest half of the skill: cutting to one idea and naming the stakes. The Message Box is a five-box worksheet — Issue (the one idea), Problems, So What?, Solutions, Benefits — where "So What?" is literally the stakes box, and the whole exercise forces everything that doesn''t serve the Issue out of the frame. It is the standard tool used to train working scientists, it is short, and it is a worksheet a student fills in about their own project, not a lecture. Use the direct PDF link given here: the site''s landing page asks for a name and email first, but this PDF downloads with no form at all.', true, 'Fetched the PDF directly (2.6 MB, HTTP 200, no form, no login); extracted cover text reads "The Message Box Workbook — Communicating Your Science Effectively — Problems? Solutions? So What? Benefits? Issue." The download landing page says "Download the full workbook so that you can start working on your communication skills anytime, anywhere," requests no payment, and the site footer states "COMPASS is a 501(c)3 tax-exempt organization."', now(), 'Real but non-disqualifying, in order of weight: 1. "Hook" is the weakest quarter of the assigned skill. "hook" and "narrative" appear zero times. The workbook teaches stakes, one-idea, and nothing-else explicitly and well; the hook is only implied by "lead with the conclusion / bottom line" and one headline-or-Tweet drill. The proposing agent was honest about this (it claimed only "the hardest half of the skill") and I agree with that framing — but a curator should not pair this resource with…', 'seed'),
    ('NestWatch Nest Monitoring Protocol', 'Cornell Lab of Ornithology', 'https://nestwatch.org/learn/how-to-nestwatch/nest-monitoring-protocol/', 'documentation', 'sampling-discipline', 'sampling discipline — why the boring schedule is the whole experiment', 'biology', 'One evening to read the protocol and code of conduct; one nesting season (~6-8 weeks, about 20 minutes every 3-4 days)…', 'none — no prior biology, no equipment, no math beyond counting', 'This is the single closest thing on the open web to the skill itself stated out loud. It does not describe sampling in the abstract — it hands a beginner a fixed visit interval and then explains, in one sentence, the trade-off that the interval exists to manage: "Longer time periods between checks make it harder to determine the outcome of the nest, while shorter intervals may increase the risk of disturbance." That is the whole lesson — the schedule is not busywork around the experiment, the schedule IS the experiment, and both drifting late and over-sampling destroy the result in different ways. It also specifies what to record on every visit regardless of whether anything changed ("the…', true, 'Honest caveat: I could not find the word "free" anywhere on nestwatch.org — I checked the protocol page, the homepage, and the About/Overview page. What I can verify by fetching is structural: the full protocol, the code of conduct, and the data-entry tutorials all render publicly with no paywall, no login wall, and no price shown at any step; the homepage says "Anyone can participate and make a difference in three easy steps"; the only money on the site is an optional donate link. Cornell Lab runs it as a public citizen-science database. Treat this as free-in-practice rather than…', now(), 'Approved, but three corrections to the proposer''s claim that a curator must carry into the catalog entry: 1. SEASONALITY — the proposer omitted this entirely and it is the most important caveat. Nest monitoring is only possible during breeding season, roughly March-August in North America. Today is September 23, so a student opening Northstar right now literally cannot start this. For more than half the calendar year this resource fails the product''s core promise of "something you CAN start…', 'seed'),
    ('eBird Rules and Best Practices', 'Cornell Lab of Ornithology', 'https://support.ebird.org/en/support/solutions/articles/48000795623-ebird-rules-and-best-practices', 'documentation', 'sampling-discipline', 'sampling discipline — why the boring schedule is the whole experiment', 'biology', '30-45 minutes to read; 4 weeks of two checklists a week to feel why the rules exist', 'none to read; to practice, you need to be able to identify a handful of local birds, which the free Merlin Bird ID app from the same lab will do for you', 'The other half of sampling discipline that NestWatch does not cover: effort metadata and the complete checklist. eBird''s rules teach a beginner that an observation is scientifically worthless unless you also record how long you looked, how far you walked, how many people were looking, and — the hard one — every species you could identify, including the boring ones. The page is explicit that you should "report all species you were able to identify by sight or sound," because a complete list encodes absence as well as presence. It also forces a choice of protocol up front (Stationary, Traveling, Area) and says to start a new checklist when the kind of sampling changes, with concrete caps…', true, 'The help article itself fetched fully with no login or paywall. eBird''s own About page states: "eBird''s free mobile app allows offline data collection anywhere in the world" and that eBird data "are freely accessible to anyone." Account creation for submitting checklists costs nothing; no payment step exists.', now(), 'Two factual overstatements in the proposing agent''s claim text — fix the blurb before shipping, though neither justifies rejecting the resource: 1. "Area" protocol: the claim says the page "forces a choice of protocol up front (Stationary, Traveling, Area)." The page does require choosing a protocol and references Stationary and Traveling, but it does NOT name "Area." The full protocol list lives on a separate linked page. Drop "Area" from the description or the catalog repeats the…', 'seed'),
    ('OpenStax Biology 2e — Section 45.1, Population Demography', 'OpenStax / Rice University', 'https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/45-1-population-demography', 'textbook', 'sampling-discipline', 'sampling discipline — why the boring schedule is the whole experiment', 'biology', '1-2 hours to read carefully and work the review questions', 'a first-year high school biology course helps but is not required; the section is self-contained and defines its terms', 'The formal vocabulary a student needs so their fieldwork sounds like ecology and not like a hobby: quadrat sampling, mark-recapture, population density, life tables. Crucially for this specific skill, it does not just name the methods — it spends real space on how each one lies to you. On mark-recapture: "Some animals from the first catch may learn to avoid capture in the second round, thus inflating population estimates. Alternatively, some animals may prefer to be retrapped (especially if a food reward is offered), resulting in an underestimate of population size." That is sampling bias taught through a concrete field failure rather than as an abstract warning, and it is exactly the…', true, 'The page states the material is published by OpenStax under a Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike license and is free to read online; the full section text rendered on fetch with no account, no trial, and no paywall. OpenStax is funded by Rice University and philanthropic foundations; the web and PDF versions cost nothing.', now(), 'One honest caveat a curator should see, and it is about fit rather than quality. The assigned skill is "sampling discipline — why the boring schedule is the whole experiment," and the emphasis in that phrasing is on temporal consistency: sampling on the same cadence, the same way, week after week, because the routine is what makes the data mean anything. This page does not teach that directly. What it teaches is spatial and methodological sampling discipline — randomize your quadrat placement,…', 'seed'),
    ('Sampling and Normal Distribution (Click & Learn)', 'HHMI BioInteractive', 'https://www.biointeractive.org/classroom-resources/sampling-and-normal-distribution', 'interactive', 'sampling-discipline', 'sampling discipline — why the boring schedule is the whole experiment', 'biology', '1-2 hours (BioInteractive classifies it as a "Long — over 50 minutes" activity)', 'none — middle school arithmetic and the ability to read a histogram; the activity builds the histogram idea as it goes', 'The quantitative answer to "why can''t I just go out once?" Students draw their own samples from a population and watch the sample distribution change as sample size changes, arriving at the conclusion themselves: "larger sample sizes are desirable when collecting data about a population, because they are more likely to reflect the true distribution." This is the piece that converts sampling discipline from a rule someone told you into something you have seen happen. It is the only resource in this set that is interactive rather than read-only, and it is built and levelled specifically for high school (the page tags it "High School — General," "High School — AP/IB," and "College"), so a 9th…', true, 'HHMI BioInteractive''s About page states: "BioInteractive is a leading provider of free life science classroom resources and professional learning for high school and undergraduate life science educators." The interactive loaded and is usable without logging in — an account is offered but not required, and no payment step exists. Hosted and funded by the Howard Hughes Medical Institute.', now(), '1. PARTIAL SKILL COVERAGE — the main reservation, and the reason this must not be the only resource for this skill. The assigned skill is "sampling discipline — why the boring schedule is the whole experiment." This resource teaches the sample-SIZE half rigorously and the PROTOCOL half not at all. Students draw samples from an already-normally-distributed simulated population; there is nothing about randomization scheme, standardized timing, consistent method, observer bias, or field protocol…', 'seed')
on conflict (url) do nothing;
